{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghuuMntvMd7NCGjpDEjd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game now has the plan's content counts and a shared-world online mode, but a hot-seat campaign for several humans is still not built.\n\nAll 361 headless checks pass. The AI Hub harness also passes at 2 players, at 4 players with latency and loss, and in the no-player-host scenario. I also ran the host-leave and 3-player cases earlier, and they passed. I haven't tested a real controller or a physical touch device.\n\n## What changed since the last report\n- **Content now matches the plan:** 25 castles, 36 knights, 30 monster species with three forms each, 13 battle maps and 40 story events.\n- **Shared-world online:** the host's copy orders every command and all copies replay the same seeded battle. The first two seats duel and the others watch. The hub tags now say shared-world for 2–4 players, so this build is no longer playable solo in a hub round.\n- **Two-human hot-seat skirmish:** the Skirmish setup can set a second human, with a hand-over screen between phases.\n- **Still missing:** a hot-seat campaign for several humans. It would mean making \"the human realm\" plural throughout the campaign rules and screens.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:26\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena. Only the title is a nod; the world, cast, art and music are original.\n- **Plan:** `PLAN.md`\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG hex-battle layer. The campaign is a diorama map, battles use a tilted hex board with a tilt toggle, and story scenes are side-on with live portraits.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge 0.85.2 (unmodified), plain JavaScript, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0. Playwright and Node are used for tests only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's own style (Humanoid knights with live portraits) plus six hand-built creature bodies, season palettes and a clock-face continent.\n- **Animation:** the engine's rig and attack moves, plus new creature animation, season effects and spell effects.\n- **Audio:** all in code: 10 songs, about 30 sound effects, and babble voices pitched per speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, as described above.\n- **Testing:** `node tests/run.mjs` (361 checks including a scripted whole-campaign playthrough and a host/client lockstep test), a balance bot, browser screenshots, and the hub harness.\n- **Research:** light: the plan, the engine card and the hub protocol.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown (Chromium via Playwright for checks).\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** nine. The main ones: the player's start was one road from Meridian, so a scripted playthrough won on turn 1; the campaign scene crashed before its first draw; the first story event fired on turn 1; deep links were overwritten by the engine's boot; the attract mode exited itself in a test; the font can't draw several symbols I had used; and the engine's corner buttons covered my HUD.\n- **Critical:** three. The Meridian shortcut and the two first-draw crashes.\n- **Most helpful:** the scripted whole-campaign playthrough plus the AI balance bot. They drove every system from the title to an ending and exposed the Meridian shortcut.\n- **Least helpful:** the data-validation suite, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** `game.advance` with `game.debug` is deterministic, so I could play the whole game in Node. The Humanoid rigs with live portraits looked good with no art work, and pause, settings, saves and button labels come free.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** the font has no controller symbols and little punctuation, so I drew the PlayStation shapes by hand. `game.go()` called before boot finishes is silently replaced by the first scene, and `game.every` timers vanish when the scene changes.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** the hex board and picking, the creature painters, a controller-first UI kit, the battle and campaign simulations with their AIs, the lockstep online layer, and the voices and songs.\n- **Hardest part:** fitting a strategy game to an engine built around a ground plane. I drew everything through `r.overlay` and did my own depth ordering and picking.\n- **Engine changes:** none.\n- **One change I'd make:** ship a small controller-first UI toolkit and a font that includes controller glyphs.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the concrete spec for controller-first play, attract mode and dev mode, and the plan's exact rules and tables.\n- **Confusing:** the plan was written for another model, effort and date, and names a different file and folder than TASK.md.\n- **Hardest thing:** writing the plan's full content in one session, and keeping a hex battle readable at 320×240. A multi-human hot-seat campaign is the one part not built.\n- **What I'd change:** mark which parts of the plan are optional, give a size budget, and make the plan's file names agree with TASK.md.\n\nFiles are in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050226__x7910ab/work`:\n- `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050226.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":972,"cache_read_input_tokens":416857,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":972},"output_tokens":718,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b041f93-88ca-4f5d-be80-9273db22fc2d","uuid":"4b1f5a9d-abed-429a-becf-e379940f681e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:47:36.219Z","request_id":"req_011CghuuMRq9QffY1jaNFzL5"}
